Sounds like you haven't bought protection from the Minetown priest. That
gives you an addition 9 points of weight-free AC. Protection is my early
game goal. I do several activities in order to secure it:

1) I let my pet level up to level 9 by letting it take care of all the
nasties. Pretty much in the early game I spend a lot of time running
monsters into my pet.

2) I clean out all the shops. Personally I'm not above credit cloning.

3) I do a preliminary ID of everything. If being spoiled doesn't bother
you then take a look at the nethack shopping spoiler and the wand
engraving spoiler. I also mark the price of everything by using shops.

4) There are three defensive tactics that are key to playing Nethack:
AC, Magic Resistance/Cancellation, and Refelction. I do my best to secure
these as best I can. AC of course in the early game. Use your pet to
sniff out non cursed stuff. Wear it. Wear everything. Usually by the
time I hit the Minetown priest I'm in dwarven mithril with a hard hat
and hard shoes. That's AC 0 or so right there.

5) Polytraps are bad news. They start occuring at Level 8 of both the main
dungeon and the Mines. If I cannot control poly or have Magic resistance
I'm very very very careful about proceeding further down.

6) And the toughest thing of all: patience. Nethack is turn based. If things
are not going well, you can stop and think for a minute. Or an hour. Or even
a day if need be. It's amazing how often you can get a DOH! momement after
dying that could have saved you if you had simply spent a minute or two thinking
about how to solve the problem. The semi-pacifist tack makes one a more careful
game player instead of just rushing into and through everything.

I beeline to the Minetown priest once I've secured the cash, the buff
pet, and the AC to withstand the first 2-3 levels of the mines. I don't
expore the mines in the first pass, just do my best to get to the stairs
and get to the priest.

Once that's done and I have my weight-free AC, I then ramp up levels a bit.
It's almost impossible not to get to level 5 just by bumping into stuff. But
after that you can slow down the pace by continuing to feed the pet 
irrelevant monsters.

> I don't have any artifacts because I haven't found a co-aligned alter,

Again, that's a level thing. You really don't need an artifact before
the Quest. A well enchanted weapon along with some well enchanted darts
(or other distance weapon or spell) can get you quite far in the game.

You do know that you can get darts/arrows from traps by disarming them right?
It makes it virtually impossible to run out of darts.

> I don't have enough stuff ID'd that could save me.

Take time to ID critical stuff. My list includes:

1) Scrolls of ID, light, EW, EA, and RC. Each are special priced in a shop.
2) Low level spellbooks. It's amazing how a 1st level spellbook can save
one's butt.
3) 175Z wands. All 4 (lightning, cold, fire, sleep) are power wands.
4) Cheap potions for conversion to holy water. I'll even convert booze until
I get a chance to ID it.
5) Spend time doing engraving ID on wands.

Each of these can be done with existing early game resources. You're pretty
much guaranteed at least one general store. Spend a few turns figuring out
what you have. 

